#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128931 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "a6d910ae-c6db-51d2-93ba-f6c823c0a8c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6179" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z= x^2 + xy$ at the point $(1,2, 3)$." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= 2x + y \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,2)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1, 2) &= 2(1) + 2 = 4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1, 2) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 3 &= (x - 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1, 2) + (y - 2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1, 2) \\\\\nz - 3 &= 4(x - 1) + 1(y - 2) \\\\\nz - 3 &= 4x + y - 6 \\\\\n- 4x - y + z + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n4x + y - z - 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128932 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "4eb4806c-3d5b-5ed2-87a1-d1ed50c267e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6182" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={y^2}+ {xy}$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nrepresents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= y \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + xy) \\\\\n&= 2y + x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0$:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = 0; \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0$. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/id #uuid "ed1a3960-c2c4-57e7-8fd1-daf5d98bf052" :question/math-academy-id "e-6185" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=x^2+y^2+z^2-2xyz$ at the point $(1,-1,1,5)$." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0,w_0)$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw - w_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0,z_0) + (z-z_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}(x_0,y_0,z_0)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} - 2xyz) = 2x - 2yz \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} - 2xyz) = 2y - 2xz \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z} &= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} - 2xyz) = 2z - 2xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (1,-1,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1, 1) &= 2(1) - 2(-1)(1) = 4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1, 1) &= 2(-1) - 2(1)(1) =-4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1, 1) &= 2(1) - 2(1)(-1) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw - 5 &= (x - 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1, 1) + (y + 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1, 1) + (z - 1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1, 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw - 5 &= 4(x - 1) - 4(y + 1) + 4(z - 1) \\\\\nw - 5 &= 4x - 4y + 4z - 12 \\\\\n- 4x + 4y - 4z + w + 7 &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 4y + 4z - w - 7 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8507d7e-07a3-5d8f-93ce-433486374489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x + 4y - z - 5 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127089} {:answer/id #uuid "dffdace6-2778-5d3d-a4cf-b5557a59f7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + 4y - z - 5 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127090} {:answer/id #uuid "10481073-7643-573d-b44e-7eadc4ee4f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8x + 3y - z - 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127091} {:answer/id #uuid "11de7225-ee75-5bfc-9dda-68fef1ce7ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 4y - z - 5 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127092} {:answer/id #uuid "39fbe8b3-8ab5-55d2-9ac4-40a876722f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x + 6y - z - 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8507d7e-07a3-5d8f-93ce-433486374489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x + 4y - z - 5 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127089} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca92eb9-afdc-5ad0-81ea-ce97504ba91d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127088}] :question/id #uuid "ab92392f-12a1-569b-997c-793bd45fc373" :question/math-academy-id "q-37595" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = y^{2}e^{2x} - 1$ at the point $(0, 2, 3)$."}]}
